Chapter 4: Existential Risk and the Fault‑Tree Constraint

4.1 Bounding Existential Risk: Ord’s Imperative

Ord (2020) argues that humanity faces roughly a one‑in‑six chance of existential catastrophe this century, and that reducing this risk to well under 0.1% per century is an overriding priority. CST adopts the target P_{\text{ex}} \le 0.001 per century as the existential security threshold. The steward’s decisions must ensure that D_R is sufficient to keep the century‑scale probability below this bound.

4.2 A Toy Fault‑Tree with Operational Proxies

We construct a fault‑tree linking observable proxies to the aggregate risk bound. Let the century‑scale probability of existential catastrophe be

P_{\text{ex}} = 1 - \prod_{j} (1 - p_j),

with j indexing major risk sources: engineered pandemics, unaligned AI, and other global catastrophic risks (climate, nuclear, asteroids). We assign maximum tolerable conditional probabilities to each source when the corresponding proxy is met:

· Pandemic early detection coverage > 80% of world population → p_{\text{pandemic}} \le 0.0002 (0.02% per century).
· Zero unaligned frontier AI models (>10^{26} FLOPs) without third‑party safety evaluation → p_{\text{AI}} \le 0.0003 (0.03% per century).
· All BSL‑4 facilities score >0.9 on independent secure‑protocol audit → p_{\text{bio}} \le 0.0002 (0.02% per century).
· Residual other risks (climate, nuclear, asteroids) capped at p_{\text{other}} \le 0.0003 (0.03% per century).

Then P_{\text{ex}} \le 1 - (0.9998 \times 0.9997 \times 0.9998 \times 0.9997) \approx 0.001, i.e., 0.1% per century. The individual p_j values are illustrative and drawn from Ord’s sensitivity ranges; in the full research programme they will be calibrated via structured expert elicitation (Chapter 8).

4.3 Correlation, Dependence, and the Independence Assumption

The product formula assumes that the risk sources are conditionally independent. This is a known weak point: dual‑use AI/bio convergence, cascading institutional failure, and correlated stressors could make the true probability higher for a given set of proxy satisfactions. We acknowledge Ord’s own caveat on this point. In the full dissertation, we will relax independence by introducing a copula‑based dependence structure, specifically a Gaussian copula with a correlation matrix estimated from expert elicitation, and compute the resulting worst‑case bound. For the present theoretical blueprint, we retain the independence assumption as a simplifying first‑order approximation and flag it for robustness testing.

4.4 Embedding the Constraint in the Steward’s Problem

The existential‑security threshold is operationalised as a state constraint: for all t, D_R(t) \ge D_R^{\min}, where D_R^{\min} corresponds to the proxy bundle that delivers P_{\text{ex}} \le 0.001. This becomes a constraint in the optimal control problem, and the dynamic weighting in the objective provides a soft penalty to guide optimisation away from violations.

