Chapter 8: Research Agenda – Future Empirical and Computational Validation

No physical experiments or computational training runs have been conducted for this dissertation. What follows is a detailed, pre‑registered plan for the future work that will test, calibrate, and potentially refute CST.

8.1 Institutional Latency and Comparative Advantage (Control‑Theoretic Model)

We will construct a formal control model with two agents: a steward with negligible decision lag and a multilateral institution with a fixed deliberation delay \tau. The environment features a bottleneck domain whose decay time is \tau_D. The analysis will identify conditions under which the steward’s speed is a net advantage (\tau_D < \tau) and conditions under which it becomes a liability (when insufficient vetting leads to catastrophic misallocation). The scenario‑robustness constraint will be incorporated, and the model will be solved to derive a critical speed‑safety frontier. This chapter will produce formal conditions that can be empirically probed.

8.2 Agent‑Based Civilisational Simulations

We will build an agent‑based model (ABM) populated by wealth holders with different allocation rules: CST‑stewardship, spend‑down philanthropy, dynasty preservation, status‑seeking, and a “democratic benchmark” where all wealth is taxed and allocated through simulated political processes. The civilisation model will include endogenous existential risk linked to the aggregate R domain stock, stochastic shocks, and cross‑domain spillovers. The simulation will run for 100‑year horizons and record: extinction events, terminal CII, bottleneck trajectories, and inequality metrics. The primary hypothesis is that CST stewards produce the lowest extinction probability and highest terminal adaptive capacity across a wide range of parameter settings. We will systematically vary the bottleneck parameter k, the initial wealth distribution, and the responsiveness of democratic institutions, mapping the conditions under which CST outperforms or underperforms. All code will be open‑sourced.

8.3 Historical Survival Analysis of Institution‑Building Philanthropy

We will construct a matched cohort study of major philanthropists (1880–1950) with inflation‑adjusted wealth above a high threshold. Each philanthropist will be classified as an “institution‑builder” (founded a durable, polycentric organisation) or a “check‑writer” (distributed funds without durable governance structures). To correct for survivorship bias, propensity‑score matching will be used on era, legal environment, initial endowment size, and sector. The primary outcome is institutional survival >50 years. Kaplan‑Meier curves and Cox proportional hazards models will test the hypothesis that institution‑builders show a significant survival advantage. The study will be pre‑registered with a detailed analysis plan, and all data sources will be made public.

8.4 Multi‑Objective Optimisation and Pareto Frontiers

We will implement the multi‑objective portfolio problem using NSGA‑III (or an equivalent many‑objective evolutionary algorithm) with the three objectives defined in Section 5.4 and the legitimacy constraints as hard filters. The output will be a set of Pareto‑optimal allocation strategies for different initial wealth levels and risk environments. These frontiers will serve as decision‑support surfaces, showing the trade‑offs between expected adaptive capacity gain, existential risk, and bottleneck severity.

8.5 CII Indicator Library Construction and Delphi Calibration

The complete indicator library will be constructed from public data sources (OECD, GCRI, V‑Dem, Harvard Flourish programme, WHO, etc.) and updated biannually. A two‑round Delphi expert elicitation will be conducted to set the internal indicator weights, the base domain weights, the bottleneck sensitivity parameter k, and the individual fault‑tree probabilities. The elicitation protocol will follow established guidelines and include experts from existential risk studies, global health, AI safety, political science, and space policy.

8.6 Legitimacy Threshold Sensitivity and Democratic Theory

A systematic sensitivity analysis will vary the HHI cap, sunset duration, and scenario‑robustness percentage across plausible ranges. We will evaluate the resulting institutional forms against normative criteria from democratic theory, including accountability, responsiveness, and the prevention of permanent plutocratic entrenchment. Structured interviews or a Delphi process with political philosophers and governance scholars will refine the thresholds. The goal is not to arrive at a single “correct” number but to map the legitimacy frontier and identify the set of minimally acceptable constraints.

