Chapter 9: Limitations and Anticipated Criticisms

9.1 The TESCREAL Challenge

CST is a longtermist, billionaire‑centred framework, and it therefore sits squarely in the crosshairs of the TESCREAL critique. We do not dismiss this criticism. We acknowledge that any framework that allows concentrated wealth to shape the long‑term future risks rationalising plutocracy. Our response is structural: CST requires democratic sunset, polycentric governance, and open‑science defaults. If these constraints prove insufficient to prevent capture, the theory will be falsified by the empirical tests. A deeper engagement with the TESCREAL literature will be integrated into the dissertation’s final chapter, including a counterfactual analysis: would the abolition of all billionaire‑driven stewardship lead to a lower existential risk, given the observed latencies of democratic institutions? This counterfactual will be explored in the ABM.

9.2 The Independence Assumption and Fault‑Tree Calibration

The toy fault‑tree assumes independence among existential risk sources. In reality, risks are correlated. The dissertation will relax this assumption using copula models; failure to bound risk below the target when correlations are realistically included would weaken the claim that the proxy thresholds are sufficient.

9.3 CII Measurement and Data Quality

Many indicators—especially the Herfindahl index of AI capability, black‑ball technology probabilities, and deep‑space supply chain diversity—are currently unmeasurable or highly uncertain. The CII will therefore be a constructed index with significant imputation and expert‑judgment artefacts. We will conduct extensive uncertainty quantification, including Monte Carlo simulations over plausible data‑generating processes, to assess the robustness of CII rankings.

9.4 The Institution‑Building Survival Study’s Data Constraints

Historical data on philanthropic institutions from 1880–1950 are sparse and subject to survivor bias. The planned propensity‑score matching can only address observed confounders; unobserved characteristics (e.g., founder charisma, luck) may remain. A null result—no survival advantage—would falsify CST’s prediction 1, but a positive result would still require careful causal interpretation.

9.5 The ABM as a Toy World

Agent‑based models of civilisation are necessarily gross simplifications. The ABM’s parameter space will be vast, and calibration will be challenging. We will conduct extensive sensitivity analysis and open‑source the model to invite adversarial scrutiny. The primary value of the ABM is to generate sharp, falsifiable conditional statements, not to predict real‑world outcomes.

9.6 Generalisability Beyond Billionaires

The theory centres on billionaires but applies analogously to any individual with exceptional resource allocation power (e.g., the head of a frontier AI lab, a sovereign wealth fund director). The dissertation will discuss the structural conditions under which the framework can be adapted to other actors.

