Appendix C: Pre‑Registration Plan for Historical Survival Analysis

Study Title: Institution‑Building Philanthropy and Institutional Longevity: A Matched Cohort Survival Analysis (1880–1950)

Background: CST’s first falsifiable prediction states that steward‑founded institutions satisfying polycentric design principles exhibit a statistically significant survival advantage (>50 years) over pure check‑writing philanthropy.

Research question: Among major philanthropists (1880–1950), is the founding of a durable, polycentric institution associated with longer institutional survival compared to donors who dispersed funds without creating a durable governance structure?

Hypothesis: Institution‑builder‑founded entities will have a significantly lower hazard of dissolution within 50 years than check‑writer‑supported entities, after controlling for era, legal environment, initial endowment size, and sector.

Data sources:

· Historical philanthropic databases: The Philanthropy Roundtable, Foundation Center historical archives, Rockefeller Archive Center, Carnegie Corporation records, Ford Foundation early records, national registers of charities.
· Wealth estimates: Forbes historical lists, The Wealthy 100 (Klepper & Gunther, 1996), probate records.
· Institutional survival data: GuideStar (for US nonprofits), Charity Commission for England and Wales, OpenCorporates, historical directories.

Inclusion criteria:

· Individuals with inflation‑adjusted net worth (in 2023 USD) exceeding $500 million at peak wealth between 1880 and 1950.
· Identifiable charitable giving or foundation creation.
· At least one philanthropic institution (foundation, trust, research institute) founded or predominantly funded by the individual.

Exclusion criteria:

· Wealth primarily dissipated before major charitable activity.
· Institutions founded after 1950.
· Philanthropists whose primary institutional vehicle was a government programme.

Classification of exposure:

· Institution‑builder: Founded at least one entity with a formal governance charter, an independent board (even if initially donor‑controlled), and a mission extending beyond the founder’s lifetime. Must have evidence of structural polycentric features (e.g., board size >3, some non‑family trustees, documented procedural rules). This classification will be done by blinded raters using a rubric based on Ostrom’s principles.
· Check‑writer: Made substantial donations but did not create a durable governance entity; funds were largely spent down during or shortly after the donor’s lifetime, or were given to existing organisations without creating a new, lasting institution.

Primary outcome: Institutional dissolution or cessation of operations within 50 years of the donor’s death (or, for living donors, within 50 years of the first major gift). For institution‑builders, the entity is the founded organisation; for check‑writers, we track the longest‑lived recipient institution that received >50% of the donor’s total giving (to avoid trivial survival).

Propensity score matching:
We will estimate a propensity score (probability of being an institution‑builder) via logistic regression using the following covariates:

· Log of inflation‑adjusted peak wealth.
· Era of peak wealth (decade fixed effects).
· Country of primary philanthropy (legal origin: English common law, French civil law, etc.).
· Sector of primary interest (education, health, science, arts, etc.).
· Political regime type at time of giving (Polity IV score).
  Matching will be 1:1 nearest‑neighbour without replacement, with a caliper of 0.2 standard deviations of the logit of the propensity score. Covariate balance will be assessed using standardised mean differences (threshold <0.1).

Statistical analysis:

· Primary analysis: Kaplan‑Meier survival curves for the matched cohorts, testing the difference using a stratified log‑rank test.
· Secondary analysis: Cox proportional hazards model with robust variance estimator to account for matching, including the exposure variable and any covariates that remain imbalanced after matching. The proportional hazards assumption will be tested using Schoenfeld residuals.
· Sensitivity analysis: E‑value analysis to assess sensitivity to unmeasured confounding.

Power calculation: Based on preliminary estimates, we expect approximately 80–100 matched pairs. Assuming a 50‑year survival rate of 40% in the check‑writer group, we will have 80% power to detect a hazard ratio of 0.55 (institution‑builder survival advantage) at alpha = 0.05.

Pre‑registration: This plan will be registered on the Open Science Framework (OSF) before any data collection begins. The final dataset, analysis code, and classification rubric will be made publicly available upon publication.

