Appendix E: Multi‑Objective Optimisation Details (NSGA‑III)

The steward’s multi‑objective problem (Chapter 5.4) will be solved using the NSGA‑III algorithm (Deb & Jain, 2014), well‑suited for many‑objective optimisation. The three objectives are:

1. Maximise expected change in CII: \mathbb{E}[C(T) - C(0)].
2. Minimise downside risk: probability that D_R violates the safety threshold during the horizon.
3. Minimise bottleneck severity: the value of k that would make the current portfolio exactly optimal if the agent were a CST‑maximiser (a measure of balance).

The decision variables are the time‑path of investment shares s_i(t) for each domain. The problem is discretised into 10‑year decision periods. The legitimacy constraints (HHI cap, open‑science, scenario robustness) are implemented as feasibility filters: any solution not meeting all constraints is assigned infinite objective values (death penalty).

NSGA‑III uses a set of well‑spread reference points to maintain diversity in high‑dimensional objective spaces. The algorithm will be run with a population of 200 individuals for 500 generations. The resulting Pareto front will be visualised using parallel coordinates and 2‑D projections. We will also report the set of solutions that are “robust” across multiple parameter perturbations (Monte Carlo filtering).

