Appendix F: Fault‑Tree Expansion and Copula Model for Correlated Risks

To relax the independence assumption of the toy fault‑tree (Section 4.3), we propose a two‑stage expert elicitation to construct a dependence model.

Stage 1: Marginal probabilities. For each risk source j, experts provide 5th, 50th, and 95th percentile estimates of the conditional probability p_j given that the CST proxy thresholds are met. These are aggregated into a probability distribution (e.g., log‑normal) for each p_j.

Stage 2: Dependence structure. Experts are asked to assess the pairwise correlation between risk sources under stressed scenarios. For example: “Given that an engineered pandemic has occurred, what is the probability that an unaligned AI incident also occurs within the same century?” This yields a correlation matrix R. The joint distribution of the p_j’s is modelled via a Gaussian copula with correlation matrix R (or a t‑copula for tail dependence). The overall existential risk is then

P_{\text{ex}} = 1 - \mathbb{E}\left[ \prod_j (1 - p_j) \right],

where the expectation is taken over the joint distribution of the p_j’s. Monte Carlo simulation will estimate this expectation and its uncertainty. The resulting bound will replace the simple product in the safety threshold check. If P_{\text{ex}} exceeds the 0.1% target under plausible correlation scenarios, the proxy thresholds will be tightened accordingly.

